OpenVIBES platform @VERSION@, offline kit for Fedora @FEDORA@ (x86_64)

This kit installs the OpenVIBES platform on a server with no network access:
the platform packages, the rule sets, and every Fedora package they need
(including PostgreSQL).

Install

  1. Copy this kit (openvibes-platform-@VERSION@-offline-fedora@FEDORA@.tar) to
     the server. To add the assistant, also copy the model file (below) into
     the same folder.
  2. Run:  tar xf openvibes-platform-@VERSION@-offline-fedora@FEDORA@.tar && sudo ./openvibes-offline/install

The installer first checks that the host is Fedora @FEDORA@ on x86_64 and that
the kit is genuine, then installs from the kit only. Nothing changes if a
check fails. The OpenVIBES package key is added to the host's rpm keys, as
with an online install. Run it again with a newer kit to update. It then opens
Setup (to open it later: sudo openvibes-admin).

The assistant's model (optional)

  File:    @MODEL_FILE@
  Link:    @MODEL_URL@
  SHA-256: @MODEL_SHA256@
  Licence: @MODEL_LICENSE_URL@

Put the file in the folder that holds the kit (or give its path to the
installer with --model FILE). The installer checks it against the SHA-256
above, refuses any other file, and stages it; open Setup (sudo openvibes-admin)
and turn on the assistant to install it. Without it the platform works as usual and
the assistant stays off.

Checking the kit by hand

  The kit is signed with the OpenVIBES package key, fingerprint
  @KEY_FINGERPRINT@
  (openvibes.gpg). SHA256SUMS lists every file and SHA256SUMS.asc is its
  signature; the installer checks both (and only does so; --check stops there).

Fedora sources

  packages/ holds unmodified Fedora packages, signed by Fedora. LICENSES/
  fedora-packages.txt lists each package with its licence and source RPM;
  sources for any of them are at https://src.fedoraproject.org/rpms/<name>
  and in Fedora's source repositories. The OpenVIBES packages are MIT
  licensed; their sources are at https://github.com/openvibes-project.
